Herpes Labialis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Prodromal tingling or burning, Clustered vesicles on erythematous base, Vermilion border location

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-016
Reason for Visit: Sudden, painful, weeping blisters on the lower lip causing extreme distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting an acute, rapidly evolving eruption of highly painful, weeping vesicular lesions on the lip. The patient is experiencing severe localized burning and significant psychological distress due to the rapid, visible disfigurement. Urgent home assessment is indicated to rapidly distinguish the condition from more serious dermatological emergencies (such as evolving Stevens-Johnson syndrome or acute bacterial cellulitis), and to initiate time-sensitive antiviral therapy within the critical 48-hour efficacy window. Delaying assessment risks a prolonged, severe outbreak and potential autoinoculation or secondary bacterial infection, fulfilling the criteria for MBS urgent after-hours item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: My lip started burning yesterday, and today it erupted into this cluster of agonizing, weeping blisters.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 24 hours ago with a distinct prodromal localized tingling and burning sensation on the lower lip following a period of intense work-related stress and lack of sleep. Over the past 12 hours, this evolved rapidly into a cluster of small, tense vesicles on an erythematous base located directly at the vermilion border of the lower lip. Several vesicles have just begun to rupture, forming highly painful, shallow ulcers. Patient notes mild, tender swelling under the jaw. Denies intraoral ulcers, high fever, target lesions elsewhere on the body, or recent new medication commencement. Reports a history of similar, less severe recurrent episodes.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High acute distress due to the rapid physical manifestation and localized pain.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 118/76 mmHg | HR: 82 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 14 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Visibly distressed, actively avoiding touching the lower face.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. Detection of Herpes Simplex Virus Type 1 via PCR swab of a ruptured vesicle would assist in confirming the diagnosis but is unnecessary for acute clinical management in this classic presentation.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam of the face reveals a 1.5cm cluster of grouped, tense, fluid-filled vesicles on a bright erythematous base situated precisely at the vermilion border of the right lower lip. Some vesicles have ruptured, exuding clear serous fluid. The surrounding skin is not diffusely indurated, hot, or excessively swollen. Intraoral examination reveals clear buccal, labial, and palatal mucosa without widespread oral involvement. Mild, tender right submandibular localized lymphadenopathy is palpable.
Environmental Observations: Safe, clean residential environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has applied lip balm from a shared container.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Herpes Labialis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Impetigo - Unlikely given the classic viral prodrome (tingling/burning), the perfectly clustered vesicular nature, and the lack of characteristic thick, honey-colored crusting (though secondary bacterial infection remains a risk).
Aphthous Stomatitis - Ruled out as the lesions are explicitly located on the exterior keratinized skin/vermilion border, whereas aphthous ulcers exclusively present on interior non-keratinized mucosa.
Herpes Zoster (Shingles) - Ruled out due to the localized, recurrent nature without a distinct, unilateral dermatomal distribution and the absence of severe neuropathic pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The explosive onset of painful facial vesicles caused significant patient alarm and warranted prompt clinical differentiation from aggressive spreading infections like staphylococcal impetigo or early facial cellulitis. The home assessment rapidly confirmed a classic, severe reactivation of Herpes Labialis. Given the timeline (within 48 hours of prodrome), immediate initiation of targeted oral antiviral therapy was strongly indicated to truncate the viral replication phase, reduce the duration of extreme localized pain, and accelerate healing. This time-sensitive intervention and strict infection control education definitively satisfy the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS 585-600 assessment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Valaciclovir 2000mg strictly twice daily for 1 day to rapidly halt viral replication. Advised topical application of a simple barrier cream (e.g., petroleum jelly) via a clean cotton swab to prevent the ulcers from cracking and bleeding during speech.
Positive Behavioral Change: To definitively prevent spreading the virus to your eyes (which can cause severe vision damage), wash your hands immediately with soap and water every single time you accidentally touch your lip. This breaks the physical chain of transmission from the active viral shedding site to vulnerable mucosal surfaces.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 7 days if the lesions begin expanding rapidly, become hot and red, or fail to form a hard crust.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the eye becomes red, painful, or sensitive to light, or if you develop a rapidly spreading, hot, red rash across your face.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical. The virus remains dormant in the nerve root and cannot be cured, only managed during outbreaks.
Clinical Course: The ruptured vesicles will form a yellowish scab over the next 3-4 days. Complete resolution without scarring typically occurs within 7 to 10 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if the sores begin leaking thick yellow pus, or if the redness spreads significantly beyond the immediate border of the lip.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The cold sore is a viral outbreak triggered by stress lowering your immune threshold. The high-dose oral antiviral medication rapidly starves the virus of the materials it needs to copy itself, shortening the painful blister phase.
Actionable Steps: Throw away any lip balms or toothbrushes used since the tingling started. Do not share cups, cutlery, or towels, and avoid kissing or close physical contact until the lesion has completely healed over with a hard scab.

